<header>
  <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue? <span class="zh">· 天空为什么是蓝色的？</span></h1>
  <p>One explanation, three audiences — English &amp; Simplified Chinese, side by side</p>
</header>

<div class="columns">

  <!-- ============ ENGLISH ============ -->
  <section class="col" lang="en">
    <div class="col-title">English</div>

    <article class="card">
      <h2>For a 3-Year-Old</h2>
      <p>Sunlight looks white, but it's actually made of all the colors mixed together, like the colors in a rainbow! When sunlight hits all the air in the sky, most colors just zoom right through. But the color blue is the bouncy one — it bounces around in the air a little and bounces into your eyes from everywhere you look. That's why the sky looks blue. 🌈</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card">
      <h2>For a High School Teenager</h2>
      <p>Sunlight is white light — a mix of all visible wavelengths. When it enters Earth's atmosphere, it interacts with gas molecules (nitrogen, oxygen) that are <em>much smaller</em> than the wavelength of visible light. This triggers <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>, where the intensity of scattered light scales roughly with <code>1/λ⁴</code>. Blue/violet light (≈400–450 nm) scatters about 5–10× more strongly than red light (≈650 nm). As a result, light arriving from the sky at your eyes is dominated by short wavelengths.</p>
      <p>Two caveats explain why it's <em>blue</em> and not <em>violet</em>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The Sun emits more blue than violet (its spectrum is a blackbody peak around ~500 nm).</li>
        <li>Violet is partly absorbed in the upper atmosphere (ozone) and human eyes are less sensitive to violet, so our brain perceives the sky as blue.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>At sunset, sunlight travels through much more atmosphere, so the blues are scattered away and you're left with the reds/oranges.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card phd">
      <h2>For a Physics PhD</h2>
      <p>The sky's color follows from inelastic (Rayleigh) scattering of solar radiation by neutral atmospheric molecules, treated perturbatively in the Rayleigh limit (<code>a/λ ≪ 1</code>, with molecular polarizability α). The differential cross-section for a single molecule is:</p>
      <span class="formula">dσ/dΩ = [k₀⁴ α² / 2π] · [(1 + cos²θ)/2] · 1/(4πε₀)²</span>
      <p>giving the familiar <code>∝ λ⁻⁴</code> dependence (for depolarized light, averaged over the two orthogonal polarization states). For air at STP, the Rayleigh optical depth is τ ≈ 0.1 at 550 nm and τ ≈ 0.25 at 400 nm — enough to tint the diffuse sky strongly without being optically thick.</p>
      <p>The full radiative transfer problem then involves:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Multi-scattering</strong>: the first-scattering approximation (which would predict a slightly brighter blue near the anti-solar point) is corrected by multiple scattering, homogenizing the color across most of the sky; the observed gradient (deeper blue at zenith) arises because longer chord lengths near the horizon remove more blue light along the line of sight.</li>
        <li><strong>Spectroscopic details</strong>: the pure Rayleigh law is modified by the depolarization ratio (air depolarizes at ~0.035, giving slight polarization of the diffuse sky — measurable and responsible for the degree of polarization peaking at 90° from the Sun) and by overlap with <strong>Raman scattering</strong> (inelastic, ~1% of Rayleigh, producing the subtle "Raman line" in scattered skylight) and <strong>Mie scattering</strong> from aerosols, which is roughly wavelength-independent and whitens the sky — this is why polluted/hazy skies look paler.</li>
        <li><strong>Source spectrum</strong>: the solar blackbody (~5778 K, modified by atmospheric absorption) combined with ozone's Chappuis and Hartley bands suppresses violet relative to blue; chromatic perception (LMS cone responses) then maps the resulting spectral power distribution to "blue."</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Near the horizon, the optical path scales as ~sec(θ) (with θ the zenith angle), so the blue optical depth grows and short wavelengths are progressively attenuated — producing the red-to-blue chromatic sweep from midday to sunset. Quantitatively, at a solar elevation of ~5°, the 450 nm path-integrated transmission is well below that of 650 nm, yielding the characteristic reds; beyond that, twilight and the "green flash" arise from the same wavelength-selective extinction combined with refraction gradients.</p>
      <p>In short: it's Rayleigh scattering (<code>λ⁻⁴</code>) off small molecules, corrected by multiple scattering, aerosol loading, molecular depolarization, source spectrum, and human color perception.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 中文 ============ -->
  <section class="col" lang="zh-CN">
    <div class="col-title">中文（简体）</div>

    <article class="card">
      <h2>给3岁小朋友</h2>
      <p>阳光看起来是白色的，但它其实是所有颜色混在一起的，就像彩虹里的颜色！当阳光照到天上的空气时，大部分颜色都直接穿过去了。但是蓝色最顽皮，它在空气里蹦蹦跳跳，从各个方向弹进你的眼睛里。所以天空看起来就是蓝色的。🌈</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card">
      <h2>给高中生</h2>
      <p>阳光是白光——可见光所有波长的混合。当它进入地球大气层时，会与气体分子（氮气、氧气）发生相互作用，而这些分子比可见光波长短得多。这会引发<strong>瑞利散射（Rayleigh scattering）</strong>，散射光的强度大致与 <code>1/λ⁴</code> 成正比。蓝光/紫光（约400–450纳米）的散射强度大约是红光（约650纳米）的5–10倍。因此，从天空进入你眼睛的光以短波长为主。</p>
      <p>有两个细节解释了天空为什么是<em>蓝色</em>而不是<em>紫色</em>：</p>
      <ul>
        <li>太阳发出的蓝比紫多（其光谱峰值在约500纳米附近）。</li>
        <li>紫光在高层大气中（臭氧层）被部分吸收，而且人眼对紫光的敏感度较低，所以大脑把天空感知为蓝色。</li>
      </ul>
      <p>日落时，阳光穿过更厚的大气层，蓝光被散射殆尽，只剩下红橙色，于是我们看到红彤彤的晚霞。</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card phd">
      <h2>给物理学博士</h2>
      <p>天空的颜色源于太阳辐射被大气中性分子的（瑞利）散射，可用小分子极化率 α 的 Rayleigh 极限（<code>a/λ ≪ 1</code>）做微扰处理。单个分子的微分散射截面为：</p>
      <span class="formula">dσ/dΩ = [k₀⁴ α² / 2π] · [(1 + cos²θ)/2] · 1/(4πε₀)²</span>
      <p>由此得到熟知的 <code>∝ λ⁻⁴</code> 依赖关系（对非偏振光，在两个正交偏振态上平均）。在标准温度和压力下，550 nm 处瑞利光学厚度 τ ≈ 0.1，400 nm 处 τ ≈ 0.25——足以让天空呈明显的蓝色，又不至于光学上"太厚"。</p>
      <p>完整的辐射传输问题还涉及：</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>多次散射</strong>：单次散射近似（会预测反太阳方向天空略亮一些）会被多次散射修正，使大部分天空的颜色趋于均匀；观察到的渐变现象（天顶方向蓝色更深）源于视线方向上较长的光程散射掉更多蓝光（近地平线方向）。</li>
        <li><strong>光谱细节</strong>：纯粹的瑞利定律会被退偏振比修正（空气的退偏振比约 0.035，使散射天光带有可测量的偏振，偏振度在与太阳成90°处达到峰值），并与<strong>拉曼散射（Raman scattering）</strong>（非弹性散射，约占瑞利散射的1%，在散射天光中产生微弱的"拉曼线"）以及气溶胶引起的<strong>米氏散射（Mie scattering）</strong>重叠；后者近似与波长无关，会使天空发白——这就是污染/雾霾天空颜色更浅的原因。</li>
        <li><strong>光源光谱</strong>：太阳的黑体辐射（约5778 K，被大气吸收修正）叠加臭氧的 Chappuis 和 Hartley 吸收带，相对削弱了紫光；色彩感知（LMS 锥细胞响应）则把最终的光谱功率分布映射为"蓝色"。</li>
      </ul>
      <p>在地平线附近，光程按 ~sec(θ) 增长（θ 为天顶角），因此蓝光的光学厚度增大，短波长被逐步衰减——从正午到日落呈现出红到蓝的色彩渐变。定量地说，当日出高度角约5°时，450 nm 的光程积分透过率远低于 650 nm，于是呈现出典型的红色；再往后，暮光与"绿闪光"则源于同样波长选择性衰减与折射率梯度共同作用的结果。</p>
      <p>简而言之：天空是蓝色，源于小分子对阳光的瑞利散射（<code>λ⁻⁴</code>），再经多次散射、气溶胶含量、分子退偏振、光源光谱以及人类色彩感知的共同修正。</p>
    </article>
